# The full game: an agent-played game of Dynasty with all eight provinces.
# Every say and aside is the reply of the agent playing that seat (see log/yearN/).
# The Storyteller writes the act lines and refs the moves; the engine keeps the books.
# The narrator's lines are not part of the game: they live in narration.md, never here.
# Each chapter opens with a "# ===== CHAPTER N: <title>" marker. Seed and deck order: seed.md.
setup([1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8], "full-game", "The full game",
      "Eight players · thirteen years · every work built",
      "All eight provinces, and a throne that changes hands at the auction table. Ten years pass without a population "
      "card and the table grows rich, while the Official's chair changes hands four times in two years and nobody will "
      "take the throne: whoever sits on it can be unseated by the next full set, reserve and all. Then the population "
      "doubles, the crown spends its whole treasury on new land for everyone, and Reina, deposed in Year 2, buys the "
      "throne back with a fifty-grain scroll. One ditch is left to dig, and digging it ends the game before anyone else "
      "can climb. In Year 13 the Empress pays for it herself, thirty grain, over the Official's objection, and the reign "
      "that began the game ends it.",
      short="8 players · 13 years")

# ============================== SETUP ==============================
R(0, "Setup", None, "All eight provinces (rulebook Section 4 and Appendix A). Every province holds four pieces of its own realia, the Warlord takes four soldiers, and every public work in the book is owed: 1,030 in all.")
act("Eight booklets: Reina Osorio draws the Emperor, Clark Grafton the Official, Richard Baron the Gentry, Noah Tall the Scholar, Silke Weber the Silk Peasant, Paddy Doyle the Rice Peasant, Graham Garner the Wheat Peasant and Robert Land the Warlord.", map_hl=list(range(1,9)))
deal_grain("Rich, the Gentry, sets out the Harvest Pile and deals 60 to each player. It is the first year's harvest.")
take_stock("Each realia province takes its four pieces of its own realia out of the box. They wait beside the board until they're sold.")
act("Clark cuts out the Province 2 Irrigation System, one square by two; Rich the Province 3 Reservoir, two by three; Graham the Province 7 Reservoir, two by two. They're set aside until built.", map_hl=[2,3,7])
act("Noah, the Scholar, shuffles the twenty I Ching cards and lays them face down.", map_hl=[4])
# From here the players' own choices, starting with Rob's army.
# ===== CHAPTER 1: Very Civilised
place_army({"W1":"G11", "W2":"M18", "W3":"O18", "W4":"T21"}, "Robert Land stands his four soldiers: W1 on G11, on the strip under the Wall between Clark's Province 2 and Rich's Province 3; W2 on M18, the desert pocket between Rich's Province 3 and Noah's Province 4; W3 on O18, the desert notch touching Noah, Silke and Graham; W4 on T21, the desert just below Paddy's Province 6.",
 "Rob: Every soldier is one step from a target. G11 can hit Clark or Rich, M18 and O18 can reach Rich, Noah, Silke and Graham, and T21 sits right on Paddy's doorstep, so from Year 1 I'm a single move away from all six people I need. The only question is who panics first and pays me 20 to buy one of them off. I'll take that money happily.")
say("Rob", "Evening, all. I've parked a few lads near your borders, purely for scenery. And Reina, darling, about the tax: the desert doesn't pay tax. The desert *is* the tax.")
act("Robert Land says he won't pay the Emperor's tax on his harvest. The Warlord may refuse it.", map_hl=[8])

# ============================== YEAR 1 ==============================
R(1, "The first year", None, "No card is drawn. The 60 each player was dealt is the first harvest, and the tax is levied on it.")
CUR_INC.update({s:60 for s in ACTIVE})
say("Rich", "Silke, Paddy, Graham, my dear friends of the soil, a mere five each, the smallest token the custom allows! And the Harvest Pile will treat you all handsomely, I promise you, to the last grain. Rob, what a charming bit of scenery. Do keep it well clear of my wheat, there's a good fellow.",
 "Rich: Fifteen a year from the fields is fifteen I don't have to earn. Asking for the minimum keeps the peasants friendly while the Warlord is the one frightening them. I'll raise it later, once they need something from me.")
rents(5, "Rents: Rich asks the minimum, and each peasant pays the Gentry 5.")
say("Reina", "Thirty percent, my friends. Moderate, and very civilised. The Summer Palace goes up this year, because the Wall and the Canal can't start without it. Rob, darling, scenery is lovely, but the desert has a habit of turning into a border, and borders get walls. Clark, Noah, five each to start. Do good work and the crown remembers.",
 "Reina: Thirty percent from six payers brings in 108. That's enough to get the Summer Palace built and the works list moving, but not enough to give anyone a revolt clause to wave at me. I'm keeping the reserve deep, because a thousand owed in works is a marathon, and Rob's four lads on G11 and M18 are pointed straight at Clark and Rich, not at me. For now.")
taxes(.30, collector="Clark, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
act("Robert Land doesn't pay.", map_hl=[8])
say("Clark", "Eighteen apiece, friends, and I'm counting it twice: Rich, Noah, Silke, Paddy, Graham, and my own eighteen first so nobody says the collector skims. That's 108 to the crown, Reina, every grain accounted for. Rob, your refusal goes in the ledger, page one, line one, in very neat handwriting.",
 "Clark: I pay first and pay in full, so when I come asking for a fatter salary than five, nobody can call me a cheat. And the Warlord's 'scenery' on G11 sits right on my doorstep, so I'm writing everything down.")
say("Reina", "The Summer Palace goes on G26, at the far eastern end of my lands, with the sea breeze and a view of the Wall. Rob, your lads would get very tired walking that far. Some scenery is best admired from a distance.",
 "Reina: G26 is about as far as I can get from all four of Rob's soldiers. F17 and I17 are open to G11 and M18, and J23 is the square his southern pair would reach first. A palace that costs me 20 every time it's raided is a liability, so I'm tucking it into the corner where it'll take him the most years to get to.")
build("Summer Palace", 20, {1:20}, key="Summer Palace", at="G26", text="Public works: Reina builds the Summer Palace on G26, the far eastern corner of Province 1. It must stand before any Wall link or the Province 1 canal.", hover="Reina pays the 20, into the Harvest Pile. 1,010 of the works are still owed.")
act("The auctions: every seat but the Emperor's gives a limit for each of the year's six lots at once, Province 2's Customs House to Province 7's Vase. Each lot is then sold as a rising auction, in order, and the top two on a close lot get one live raise each.", map_hl=[2,3,4,5,6,7])
say("Clark", "My Customs House opens at two, friends, a bargain from a public servant. Fair warning: I'm bidding modestly and I'm writing down every grain anyone burns on a bidding war, because that's grain the crown should have taxed.",
 "Clark: Forty-two grain, five pieces to collect and twenty to keep back, so I'll take three cheap lots this year and pick up the rest when the market's calmer. Everyone else is poorer than me after tax, so the bidding should stay low.")
say("Rich", "Splendid tax, Your Majesty, so moderate, so civilised, I'd have paid it twice. Friends, I'll bid politely on everything and fight over nothing. Anyone who'd rather settle a piece over a cup of tea than in the auction hall, my door is always open.",
 "Rich: Five pieces to buy and 57 grain, so I keep every ceiling low and let the others bleed into the Harvest Pile. Whatever I miss this year will cost the same next year, once they've run out of grain.")
say("Noah", "Before anyone gets excited, the arithmetic: four of each piece, five of us who need it. That means one of us goes without every time, and each grain over the minimum puts three quarters in the Pile and a quarter in the seller's pocket. So I'll bid small, and if two of us want the same piece I'd rather settle it at the table than in a bidding war. My Bamboo Brushes open at two, the honest minimum.",
 "Noah: I'm going for the first three pieces cheap and leaving the Bowl and Vase for later years, so I keep enough grain for tax and the land squares once the population doubles. If someone outbids me at six, they've paid the Harvest Pile for the privilege, and I'm happy to let them.")
say("Silke", "Eighteen to the crown, five to the landlord, and I'm left counting silk threads. I'll bid small and honest, friends, and I'd take it kindly if nobody turns a two-grain bowl into a ten-grain war. Paddy, Graham, we're all coast and furrow; let's not feed the Harvest Pile each other's dinners. And my Dragon Jars are at the floor for anyone who wants one.",
 "Silke: I've got thirty-seven grain and five pieces to buy over ten years, so I'm putting low ceilings on everything and paying a little more only for my fellow peasants' Bowl and Vase. Any lot that goes past a fair price can wait for next year, and I'll keep a close eye on Rob's soldier on O18, sitting right on my doorstep.")
say("Paddy", "Ah, the rent's paid and the tax is paid, and I've still a few bowls of rice to my name, so I'll put in a quiet hand on each of these. Nothing wild, mind you. If anyone's desperate for a piece, say so now and I'll stand aside for first claim on the next one. My Wooden Bowls go at two, a fair price for an honest bowl.",
 "Paddy: Thirty-seven grain and five things to buy, so I'm keeping every bid small and spreading them out. Anything I win cheap is a step ahead, and if I lose I've kept grain for next year's tax. Rob's lad on T21 is sitting right below my paddies, so I'm watching him more than the auction.")
say("Graham", "Five to Rich and eighteen to Clark, and the wheat hasn't been cut yet. Fine. I'll pay fair prices for fair goods, no more. My Vase goes at two to anyone who needs it. Rob, O18 is a lovely spot for scenery. It's also eight squares from my nearest field, and I've counted them.",
 "Graham: Four apiece on everything, so if I take the lot it's twenty, and I keep the rest back for land when the population doubles. Twenty-three gone before the auction opens, and I'm writing down who took it.")
act("Rob's W3 on O18 is in fact one step from O17, Graham's nearest square. Eight is the distance from W1, on G11.", map_hl=[7,8])
say("Rob", "Bid away, friends, buy all the pretty jars and bowls you like. I'll admire them up close soon enough. And Reina, a palace out by the sea? How thoughtful. My lads love a long walk.",
 "Rob: I don't need a single piece of this, I need *them* to own it. Every Vase and Dragon Jar that lands on the board tonight is a target for one of my soldiers, and they're paying to set the table for me.")
act("Every opening minimum is 2. Rob passes on every lot. On all six, the top two limits finish level or a grain apart, so Rich, Clark, Noah and Silke each get one live raise.", map_hl=[2,3,4,5,8])
say("Rich", "Clark, the Bowl is yours, with my compliments. I'd never bid a public servant up a single grain. Noah, Silke, I'll go one grain higher and not a hair more, that's my word, and if you beat me I'll applaud.",
 "Rich: I'm giving Clark the Bowl so he owes me a kindness, and I'm only going one grain up on the rest. If they outbid me, they've paid the Harvest Pile for the privilege, and I've kept my grain for land.")
say("Clark", "One grain more on the Deed and the Bowl, and not a grain past that. Noah, you said it yourself: every grain over the minimum feeds the Pile. Rich, enjoy the Brush, it's a fine thing to sign receipts with.",
 "Clark: Ties go to my seat, so one grain each covers the obvious one-grain raise and still keeps me well clear of my twenty in reserve. I'm letting the Brush go to Rich. That's a favour, and I'll be asking for it back.")
say("Noah", "Three ties, and I lose all three on seat order. That's what the Scholar gets for sitting fourth. I'll go a grain or two higher on the Customs House and the Deed and no further. Rich, the Dragon Jar is yours, and I'm pointing it out so nobody says I stood aside quietly.",
 "Noah: There are four of each piece and five of us who need it, so coming away empty in Year One is how you end up the one left out. I'm paying a little over the odds for two pieces and giving up the third, so my grain stays above thirty.")
say("Silke", "Rich, you've got fifty-seven grain and a house full of deeds. I've got thirty-seven and an empty shelf. Let a poor weaver have one Vase this year, and you're welcome to the next one at the floor price.",
 "Silke: I came out of every other lot with nothing, so going two over for the Vase is worth it to get something on the shelf in Year 1. I'm hoping Rich's 'fight over nothing' talk means he stands at five.")
DC = "Take it, then. It's noted in the ledger, at a price I wouldn't have paid."
DR = "Oh, take it, take it, with my blessing. Another one will come along."
DN = "Too rich for a Scholar. Enjoy it; I'll be counting."
DS = "Too rich for a silk weaver. Enjoy it, I'll be buying next year."
DP = "Go on so, it's yours. I'll catch the next one."
DG = "Take it. Somebody had to pay too much for it, and it wasn't going to be me."
lot(2, "CH", [B(5,4), B(5,None,DS), B(7,4), B(7,None,DG), B(6,5), B(6,None,DP), B(3,7), B(3,None,DR), B(4,8)],
 hover=TH(("", "Rich raised to 7, Noah to 8."),
          ("Noah", "L22 is out of every soldier's reach for two years, so Rob's lad on M18 can stare at it all he likes. I've got one piece and about 34 grain, with four pieces still to buy.")), at="L22")
say("Noah", "Eight grain for the Customs House. Six of it goes to the Pile and two to Clark, so I've paid tax twice this year and I'm saying so first. I'm putting it in the middle of my province, L22, where nobody can just walk up to it.")
lot(3, "LD", [B(5,4), B(5,None,DS), B(6,4), B(6,None,DP), B(7,4), B(7,None,DG), B(4,6), B(2,7), B(4,None,DN)],
 hover=TH(("", "Clark and Noah both raised to 7, and a tie goes to the earlier seat: Clark, in Province 2."),
          ("Clark", "Both pieces sit deep in the corner no soldier can reach for two years, so W1 on G11 can sniff around my border and find nothing. And Rich gave up the Bowl politely, which means he's waiting for something in return; noted.")), at="J15")
lot(4, "BB", [B(5,4), B(5,None,DS), B(6,4), B(6,None,DP), B(7,4), B(7,None,DG), B(2,5), B(2,None,DC), B(3,6)],
 hover=TH(("", "Clark stood at 5."),
          ("Rich", "Both pieces go in the middle of Province 3, where none of Rob's soldiers can reach them for two years. I've lost the Customs House to Noah, but Silke owes me a Vase at two grain, and I'll be collecting it.")), at="K12")
lot(5, "DJ", [B(6,4), B(6,None,DP), B(7,4), B(7,None,DG), B(4,5), B(4,None,DN), B(3,6)],
 hover="Rich raised to 6; Noah stood at 5. Rich puts the Jar on L12, beside his Brush.", at="L12")
lot(6, "WB", [B(7,4), B(7,None,DG), B(5,5), B(5,None,DS), B(3,5), B(3,None,DR), B(2,6)],
 hover="Clark raised to 6; Rich stood at 5, as he said he would. Clark puts the Bowl on H15, beside his Scroll.", at="H15")
say("Clark", "The Deed goes in the strongbox at J15 and the Bowl on the back shelf at H15, both well inland. Rob, your scenery is welcome to look, but there's nothing on my front porch to admire.")
say("Rich", "Noah, Silke, thank you both, a brush and a jar, and they'll sit pride of place in the very heart of my estate. Silke, my dear, I'll hold you to that Vase at the floor price next year, with the greatest affection. Rob, you'll have to admire these from a very long way off.")
lot(7, "VA", [B(6,5), B(6,None,DP), B(3,6), B(3,None,DR), B(5,7)],
 hover=TH(("", "Rich raised to 6, Silke to 7."),
          ("Silke", "P26 is the deepest corner of my coast, out of every soldier's reach for at least two rounds, so the one piece I've paid for is about as safe as it can be. Seven grain was steep, but I'd rather have one piece safe than none.")), at="P26")
say("Silke", "Thank you, Rich, and thank you, Graham. This Vase is going right out by the sea where the silk dries, as far from the desert as my land goes. Rob, your lads are welcome to admire it from O18. Squint hard.")
act("Only Graham sells Vases, and he sets each one's minimum. Silke's offer of the next Vase at the floor price can only mean she won't bid against Rich for it. Paddy and Graham bid on every lot open to them and come away with nothing.", map_hl=[3,5,6,7])
march({"W1":"H10", "W2":"L17", "W3":"O19", "W4":"S22"}, "Robert Land marches: W1 steps off the strip onto H10 and W2 up from the desert onto L17, both in Rich's Province 3; W3 steps onto O19 in Silke's Province 5; W4 steps up onto S22 in Paddy's Province 6. Four raids, five each, and no river crossed.",
 "Rob: Every step this round is a raid with no toll, so that's 20 grain in my pocket, and each lad is now closer to a piece: W1 and W2 are closing on Clark's and Rich's strongboxes, W3 is pointed at Noah's Customs House, and W4 is wading through Paddy's rice towards Silke's seaside Vase. I'm saving the fifth soldier's fee until someone's nervous enough to start buying mine off.")
say("Rob", "Knock knock! Rich, Silke, Paddy, five apiece please, that's the going rate for the pleasure of my company. Clark, your front porch is empty, so I'm coming round the back.")
act("Two soldiers raided Rich's province, so Rich pays 10, not 5. Rob hires no fifth soldier this year.", map_hl=[3,8])
salaries({2:5, 4:5}, text="Salaries: Reina pays Clark and Noah 5 each, as she declared.")

# ============================== YEAR 2 ==============================
R(2, "The rains hold off", "dribble1")
act("Noah turns the first I Ching card: Bad Harvest. Every province collects a bad harvest this year, and the population holds at x1, 100 million.", map_hl=[4])
say("Noah", "The I Ching says: 'Bad Harvest. The seed rotted, the rains came late, and the ox went lame. All provinces collect a bad harvest.' The population holds at one hundred million, multiple one, so read the bad-harvest row at x1, every one of you. Reina, a thin year is a bad year for a heavy tax. Thirty percent of very little is still very little, and the Wall gets built by people who've eaten.",
 "Noah: A bad harvest everywhere hits the peasants hardest, and they're already on 26 to 33 grain after rent and Rob's visits. I'm nudging the Emperor towards a light tax before she says a number, because a starving coast can't buy realia or pay for the works.")
harvest("Rich, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
say("Rich", "The harvest is paid out exactly as the Pile commands, friends: ten to each of you and every grain counted, and Your Majesty's ten goes back into the Pile, I'm afraid. Rent stays at the humble five in a bad year. I'm not a monster, I'm a landlord. Rob, two of your lads called on me at once. I do hope you'll send a thank-you note.",
 "Rich: Rob took ten off me, so the rent puts fifteen back and keeps the peasants on my side. Raising it in a bad year would just push them towards the Warlord. What I still need is the Customs House, the Bowl, the Vase and twenty grain, and I'll save hard for them.")
rents(5, "Rents: Rich keeps the rent at the minimum, and each peasant pays the Gentry 5.")
say("Reina", "Noah's right, a lean year calls for a light hand: twenty percent. And Graham, you bid on everything and came home with nothing, so the crown is building you a reservoir. Sixty grain from my own purse, plus twenty a year on your harvest from now on. Rob, do try not to drink it.",
 "Reina: Twenty percent of a bad harvest is only 12 grain, so I'm not going to squeeze anyone over it. The real move is the 60 I'm putting into Graham's reservoir: it's the biggest bite I can take out of the works I owe, and it buys me a grateful wheat farmer sitting right where Rob's W3 is breathing down his neck. A peasant who's been fed doesn't sign a revolt, and I'm still left with about 70 in reserve.")
taxes(.20, collector="Clark, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
act("Robert Land doesn't pay; his bad harvest left him nothing to tax in any case.", map_hl=[8])
say("Clark", "Two grain apiece, friends, and twelve to the crown. Noah's right that it's a lean year, but the ledger doesn't care how lean it is. Rob, you've collected twenty in raids and paid the crown nothing. That's line two, right under line one.",
 "Clark: Twelve grain is barely worth the walk, but the Official who collects every grain gets to ask for more than five when the fat years come. And Rob is twenty up on the crown and climbing, so somebody had better start counting what that costs us.")
build("Province 7 Reservoir", 60, {1:60}, key="res7", at="O16:P17", text="Public works: Reina builds the Province 7 Reservoir, two squares by two, on O16 to P17, the corner of Graham's province nearest Rob's soldiers. It adds 20 to Graham's harvest from next year.", hover="Reina pays the 60, into the Harvest Pile. Graham chose the site. 950 of the works are still owed.")
say("Clark", "The Customs House stays at two, friends. Rich, last year I let the Brush go to you without a fight, and I've written it down, so I'm asking for it back now: stand aside on one Vase for the Official. Paddy, Graham, Noah, I'll go up to seven on what I need and not a grain more, so don't waste your dinners trying to outbid me.",
 "Clark: Three pieces left, and seven a piece keeps my twenty in reserve even if I win the lot. Calling in Rich's favour in public means it costs him face to say no.")
say("Rich", "What a generous crown, Your Majesty, a reservoir for Graham! I'm moved to tears. Friends, I'm bidding on three pieces only this year: the Customs House, the Bowl and the Vase. I'll leave the rest of you entirely in peace, and anyone who'd rather settle over tea than bid, just say the word.",
 "Rich: I only need three pieces now, so I'm saying so publicly and staying out of every other lot. The higher ceilings are a sign that I mean it, and I'll still have over thirty grain left for the twenty I need.")
say("Noah", "Arithmetic first: three of each piece left and four of us needing each one, so somebody comes home empty every time, and last year that was Paddy and Graham on every lot. I'm bidding on the Deed and the Jar and putting a small hand on the Bowl. I'm staying out of the Customs House, which I already have, and the Vase. Brushes open at two, as before.",
 "Noah: I spend at most twenty on the two pieces I lost on seat order last year, and I keep over twenty in hand for land when the population doubles. The Bowl at five is only there in case nobody else turns up.")
say("Silke", "Rent, tax, and a soldier on my doorstep asking five for the privilege. I'm down to twenty-nine and a half, so my bids are small and they stay small. Rich, as promised, I'm not bidding on a Vase this year, so Graham's go to the rest of you without me. Rob, if your lad on O19 wants silk, he can buy it like everyone else.",
 "Silke: I have the least grain of anyone at the table, so I've spread small, honest ceilings over the four pieces I still need and kept my word to Rich by passing on the Vase. If I come home with one or two at a fair price, that'll do; the rest can wait for a fatter year.")
say("Paddy", "Well, I bid on every lot last year and came home with an empty cart, so I've learned my lesson. I'm going properly for the Deed and the Dragon Jar this time. Silke, Graham, if either of you lets me have one of those two quiet-like, I'll stand aside for you on the Customs House and the Brush, you have my word. Bowls stay at two.",
 "Paddy: Last year's prices ran six to eight and my fours and fives got nothing, so I'm putting my real money on two pieces and keeping the rest low in case I get lucky. Rob's lad is already standing in my paddies at S22, so whatever I win is going up by Q19 where he can't reach.")
say("Graham", "Thank you, Reina. The reservoir goes at O16 to P17, in the corner Rob's lads keep looking at. Let them look at water. Last year I bid on everything and went home with an empty cart, so this year I'm bidding seven, plainly, and I'd ask Paddy and Silke to let the furrow have one piece before Rich gets his second shelf. My Vases are four now. Rich, you can afford it.",
 "Graham: Water on the corner they can reach keeps the safe squares in the middle free for anything I buy. And the Vase price goes up for the man who's taken my rent twice and given me ten back.")
say("Rob", "Go on, Paddy, Graham, buy something this year, I beg you. My lads are standing in your fields with nothing to carry home. And Reina, a reservoir! I'll bring a cup.",
 "Rob: Paddy and Graham are the two names on my list with nothing on the board, so I'm cheering for them to win a lot. Once their pieces land, my soldiers are already standing on their doorsteps.")
act("Every minimum is 2 but Graham's Vase, at 4. Rob passes on every lot. No lot reaches 10, so the limits settle each one with no live raise.", map_hl=[2,3,4,5,6,7,8])
DC = "Have it, then. I've noted the price, and the Pile thanks you for your generosity."
DR = "Well bid, well bid. I bow to the better purse."
DN = "Gone past my number. I'll leave it there and be glad of the grain."
DS = "Go on, take it. A weaver knows when the thread's too dear."
DP = "Fair play to you, it's yours. I'll have the next one."
DG = "Go on, then. I'll remember the price."
