say('Noah', "Thank you, Graham, and congratulations on the seat. That was well earned, and I lost the Bowl to you by one grain fair and square. The Vase goes on J22, beside the Jar. For the record, I now need one Bowl and twenty grain, and I've ninety-four, so nobody need wonder what I'll bid on next year. Paddy, the favour stands whenever you want it.")
say('Reina', "The Customs House goes on L9 and the Brush on M9, side by side, a long way from anybody's boots. Graham, congratulations, darling; you played the auction hall the way Rich taught us all. Clark, welcome to the wheat. The furrows are friendly and the rent is five.",
 "Reina: That's three of my five: the Bowl, the Customs House and the Brush, all tucked up under the Wall where Rob can't reach them for two years. There's one Jar left in Silke's cart, and Noah and Clark have both promised to stay out of it, so after that all I need is a Vase.")
say('Graham', "Customs House, Deed, Brush, Jar, Bowl, and forty-five and a half grain. That's the set. Clark, you took the Brush from me on a tie because of where you sit. I said I'd remember the price. I'll take the seat, and the pen.")
swap(7, 2, "Graham Garner shows his complete set (Customs House, Land Deed Scroll, Bamboo Brush, Dragon Jar, Wooden Bowl) and 45.5 grain, and replaces Clark Grafton as the Official, in Province 2. He keeps his grain and takes all of Clark's. His Customs House goes back to Province 2's stock; his Deed, Brush, Jar and Bowl move with him to G13, G14, G15 and G16. Clark returns every piece he holds to stock, the Land Deed he has just won among them, draws 60 from the Harvest Pile, and plays the Wheat Peasant, in Province 7, from now on.",
 hover=TH(("", "Section 11: a peasant may replace the Official, Gentry, Scholar or another peasant, never the Emperor. Graham's new objective is the Official's: one of every type but the Customs House, plus 20, so he needs a Vase."),
          ("Graham", "Province 2 is the lowest seat I'm allowed to take, it comes with seventy-eight grain, and the Official's job is collecting everybody's tax, Rich's crown included. All I need now is a Vase, and I've watched every one of those go for less than a Deed.")),
 at={"LD":"G13", "BB":"G14", "DJ":"G15", "WB":"G16"})
say('Graham', "Clark, the reservoir's on O16. Look after it; it was kind to me. Rob, there's a nice clean line on page one, and it's mine now.",
 "Graham: All four are on the G column, out of every soldier's reach this year and next, so I've only to sit tight, find a Vase and keep twenty grain.")
march({"W1":"I9", "W2":"I15", "W3":"L22"}, take={"W3":("CH","F8")}, text="Robert Land marches, his legs keeping him out of Provinces 5, 6 and 7 this year: W3 steps onto L22 in Noah's Province 4 and takes his Customs House instead of the 5, setting it on F8 beside Clark's old Deed. W1 steps onto I9 in Reina's Province 3, and W2 onto I15 in Province 2, Graham's now; Reina and Graham pay 5 each. W4 and W5 stand still.",
 hover="Rob: Two pieces down, Clark's and Noah's, and my legs only stop me in the south, so the northern lads are still busy. W1 sits one step off Reina's Bowl and W2 is two off Graham's whole shiny row on the G-file, so watch those two next year.")
say('Rob', "Noah, you said you'd noticed. Good. Then you won't be surprised your Customs House is off to the desert to keep my Deed company. Graham, congratulations on the pen. My boy's just stepped into your new garden to say hello.")
salaries({2:8, 4:6}, text="Salaries: Rich pays the Official 8, now Graham, and Noah 6, as he declared.")

# ===== CHAPTER 3: A Price for a Throne
# ============================== YEAR 5 ==============================
R(5, "The Emperor's Share", "share")
act("Noah turns the I Ching card: The Emperor's Share. Every province collects an average harvest, the population holds at x1, 100 million, and every province with a public work splits that work's extra harvest 50/50 with the Emperor this year.", map_hl=[4])
say("Noah", "Year Five's card is \"The Emperor's Share\": \"The Emperor would like to remind you who paid for the ditch.\" Every province collects an average harvest, and any province with a Public Works splits the extra it produces fifty-fifty with the Emperor this round. The counter stays at 100 million, so that's multiple x1; read the x1 row of your income table. Nobody plays a personal disaster this year. And Rich, I'll take my five at the end of the year, thank you.",
 "Noah: An average year is a quiet year, so the only question at this table is where Rob's boots go. The squares from J19 to M19 are safe this year and next, and that's where my next pieces are going.")
harvest("Reina, the Gentry, pays out the average harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
act("The Emperor's Share: the Province 7 Reservoir, the only public work outside the Emperor's own province that adds to a harvest, gave Clark an extra 20 this year, so Clark pays Rich half of it, 10. Rich's own canals in Province 1 are already the crown's.", deltas={2:-10, 3:10}, map_hl=[1,7])
say("Reina", "Thirty-five to everyone, and Clark darling, your fifty-five, minus Rich's ten for the ditch. The rent stays at five again this year, the same as always, for as long as the furrows and I stay friends. And Silke, darling, I haven't forgotten that last Jar. Noah said it in front of all of us, and the minutes remember.",
 "Reina: Five grain from each peasant costs me nothing, and it means no peasant has a reason to sign a revolt against me, which matters more now that I'm two pieces off the throne. I'm watching Graham, who has the most grain at the table and a Jar already, so whatever Silke puts up next, I need to win it outright and not by one.")
rents(5, "Rents: Reina keeps the minimum, and each peasant pays her 5.")
say("Rich", "Thirty again, my friends. It's moderate, it's civilised, and it's not a grain more. Noah, your five has become six, the same as last year, because a Scholar who reminds the Emperor who paid for the ditch should be paid for his candour. Graham, your eight, with my thanks for collecting it. And this year I'm building something small, a Winter Palace, so that the canals can one day come down to the coast and Silke and Paddy can split their extra with me too. Rob, I'm told the desert is lovely in winter. The nine you owe will keep.",
 "Rich: Twenty grain buys me the right to dig canals into three more provinces, and every one of them pays the crown half its extra, so it's a cheap palace that earns its keep. Meanwhile I'm watching Graham: Scroll, Brush, Jar and Bowl in a neat row on his squares and Customs Houses in his pocket. All he's missing is a Vase, and Clark has two of those and a long memory, so I keep the granary at a hundred and eighty for the evening Graham knocks on my door.")
act("The Emperor's Share is this year's card only. A canal built later in Provinces 4, 5 or 6 adds its 10 a section to that province's own harvest; it pays the crown half only in a year that draws this card.", map_hl=[4,5,6])
say("Rich", "H21, then, tucked well back from the Wall and out of the desert's reach. And I'm corrected, Noah, with thanks: the ditch only pays the crown in a year that draws your card, so a coastal canal would fatten Silke's and Paddy's harvests and not mine. A sovereign can live with that. It makes the palace a gift and not a toll, and I'm happy to be known for gifts.",
 "Rich: H21 is out of every soldier's reach this year and next, so it can't cost me a raid fine of 20, and it isn't on the edge squares where realia would want to go. The canals won't pay me directly any more, but a canal is still a public work off the 710, and a grateful coast is worth more than ten grain.")
taxes(.30, collector="Graham, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
act("Robert Land doesn't pay his 3.", map_hl=[8])
say("Graham", "Thirty percent. Ten and a half each from Reina, Noah, Silke, Paddy, and from me, since I don't excuse myself. Sixteen and a half from Clark, because the Reservoir paid you fifty-five and the ten you gave Rich came out of the harvest, not out of the tax. Sixty-nine in all, counted twice and handed to the crown. Rob, I've got you down as refusing again, so that's last year's nine plus this year's. I'm not chasing you. I'm only writing it down.",
 "Graham: Collecting the tax doesn't win me anything. What it gets me is a clean ledger, and I want everyone to see that the man with the pen pays his own share in full. I need one Vase. Silke's is on P26 and Clark has two he hasn't sold, so the next time a Vase comes up for auction I'd like the table to already think of me as someone who pays.")
build("Winter Palace", 20, {1:20}, key="Winter Palace", at="H21", text="Public works: Rich builds the Winter Palace on H21, in Province 1. It must stand before any canal in Provinces 4, 5 and 6.", hover="Rich pays the 20, into the Harvest Pile. 690 of the works are still owed.")
act("The auctions: every seat but the Emperor's gives a limit for each of the year's six lots at once, from Province 2's Customs House to Province 7's Vase, and each lot is sold as a rising auction, in order. Graham now sells Province 2's Customs Houses and Clark Province 7's Vases.", map_hl=[2,3,4,5,6,7])
say("Graham", "I'm not bidding on anything until the Vase comes up, and everyone here knows why. Reina, Paddy, here's a public offer, and it's fair. If you stand aside on Clark's Vase, the next time a piece you need is up I won't bid on it, and I'll put that in the ledger in front of all of you. If you won't stand aside, I'll take it anyway, and I'll remember what it cost.",
 "Graham: Win the Vase and I've got the full set and more than twenty grain to spare, so I can top a ceiling of a hundred and twenty-five and still meet the grain I need. Reina is the only one rich enough to make it hurt, and she still needs the Jar, so I'm watching to see if she spends on that first.")
say("Reina", "Graham darling, that's a lovely offer, and you'd have to think I can't count to make it. You hold four of five, and that Vase is your fifth, so asking me to stand aside on it is asking me to hand you the chair. Thank you, no. Silke, I'm keeping Noah to his word on your Jar, and Clark, as far as I'm concerned you and I settled that matter in Year 4.",
 "Reina: Graham's Vase is the whole game: if he gets it he has all five pieces and the grain to take the throne, so I'll go to nearly everything I have to stop him, after paying for the Jar the table promised me. If I get both, I'm the one with all five pieces, and Rich and I can have our civilised conversation.")
say("Noah", "I'll give you my numbers, since I'll be saying them anyway. I've 127, I need a Customs House and a Bowl, and I have to keep 20 back for the throne, so that's 107 to spend between the two lots and I won't go a grain past fifty on either one. I'm keeping my word on Silke's Jar and staying out of it for Reina. Paddy, I still owe you that favour. If you want the Customs House badly, outbid me and I won't hold it against you, but I'm not standing aside on it this year.",
 "Noah: Fifty plus fifty leaves me 27, which is seven more than the 20 I need to sit in the chair, so if both lots fall my way I'm one honest step from the throne. I'm staying out of Graham's Vase fight completely. Whoever wins that one pays for it, and I'd rather it wasn't me.")
say("Silke", "Noah, keep your Customs House and your Bowl, I won't make you pay my price for them. But that Deed in Reina's cart is the last one there is, and I've lost it by one grain and by six. Paddy, Clark, I'm telling you to your faces: I don't lose it a third time. Reina, your Jar's at fifteen. The minutes can remember that too.",
 "Silke: Everything I have goes on the one lot nobody can ever sell me again. If I lose the last Deed I'm finished, so I'd rather go hungry. Customs Houses and Bowls come round again, and Noah has the purse for them this year anyway.")
say("Paddy", "Silke, I heard you, and I'll not go forty on that Deed again. Once was enough for my nerves. But I'm not handing it over for nothing either. Graham, it's a fair offer you made, but a man who's one Vase off the chair doesn't get my Vase handed to him. My Bowls start at five, Noah, and I hope you get one cheap.",
 "Paddy: I've near a hundred grain and four pieces still to find, so I'm putting a sensible price on each and no more, and I'm keeping something back for the new land. The one place I'll stretch is the Vase, because if Graham walks off with it he's in the chair and we're all paying for it.")
say("Clark", "For the minutes: I'm out of Silke's Jar, as I promised in Year 4, because Reina still hasn't got one. Graham, you took my seat, my pen and my forty-one-grain Deed, and now you want my Vase to finish the set. The Vase starts at forty, and that's not spite, it's arithmetic. Page one, line five.",
 "Clark: Graham can only win the chair through my Vase, so this year I set the price, and a quarter of every grain he pays for it comes to me. On the rest I'm picking up whatever goes cheap and letting Silke and Noah fight over the pieces they care about.")
say("Rob", "Go on, all of you, bid each other up. Every pot you pay forty for is one I'll be collecting for free. Graham, I don't mind who gets the Vase, but if it ends up in that tidy little row of yours, W2 will be very pleased to see it. Do put it somewhere pretty.",
 "Rob: Anything bought at auction counts for nothing on my card, so I'm keeping all hundred and forty-five grain and letting them spend theirs. Every lot that sells ends up on the board, and that's my shop window. W2 is two steps from Graham's row, so whatever he buys, he's only minding it for me.")
act("Minimums: Graham's Customs House 2, Reina's last Land Deed 2, Noah's Brush 2, Silke's last Jar 15, Paddy's Bowl 5, Clark's Vase 40. Rob passes on every lot. No lot comes out close enough for a live raise.", map_hl=[2,3,4,5,6,7,8])
DC = "Too rich for a wheat farmer. Noted, and carried forward."
DR = "Well, darling, enjoy it. It'll cost you more than it's worth, and I keep careful books."
DP = "That's past what it's worth to a rice farmer. Go on, it's yours."
lot(2, "CH", [B(7,12), B(7,None,DC), B(6,14), B(6,None,DP), B(4,15)],
 hover="Graham's minimum was 2. Noah puts the Customs House on J19.", at="J19")
lot(3, "LD", [B(7,10), B(7,None,DC), B(6,22), B(6,None,DP), B(5,23)],
 hover=TH(("", "Reina's minimum was 2. This was the last Land Deed Scroll in the game's stock. Silke had said she'd go to 64 for it."),
          ("Silke", "O21 and P21 are the only two squares of mine no soldier can reach this year or next, so the Deed I waited five years for stays put. I've three of five now, and once the Customs House and the Bowl come round I'll stop being the cheapest person at this table.")), at="O21")
lot(4, "BB", [B(7,10), B(7,None,DC), B(6,12), B(6,None,DP), B(5,13)],
 hover="Noah's minimum was 2. Silke puts the Brush on P21.", at="P21")
say("Silke", "Twenty-three for the last Deed and thirteen for a Brush. Five years of losing, and the thread turns out cheaper than I was afraid it would be. Thank you, Paddy, for your nerves. Rob, they're both well back from the sand, so your lads can admire them from where they are. And Graham, a hundred and twenty-three for a Vase. I hope the chair is comfortable at that price.")
lot(5, "DJ", [B(3,15)],
 hover=TH(("", "Silke's minimum was 15, and Reina was the only bidder: Noah and Clark stayed out, as they had promised."),
          ("Reina", "The Jar goes on L10 next to my Customs House and Brush, behind the Wall where nobody's soldiers reach it for two years, so I'm one Vase from the full set. Graham has the full set now but he's nearly out of grain, so one raid on that row in the north and he's back to bidding, and I still have more grain than him.")), at="L10")
say("Reina", "Thank you, Silke darling, and thank you Noah and Clark. Fifteen it is, and that's a promise kept in front of everyone. Graham, a hundred and twenty-three for a Vase. You'll have all five pieces and about thirty grain left. I'd count those thirty very carefully, darling, and so will Rob.")
lot(6, "WB", [B(7,8), B(7,None,DC), B(4,9)],
 hover="Paddy's minimum was 5. Noah puts the Bowl on K19.", at="K19")
act("With the Customs House and the Bowl, Noah holds the Scholar's full set: a Customs House, Land Deed Scroll, Dragon Jar, Wooden Bowl and Vase, with 106 grain. It is complete after the sixth lot, before Clark's Vase is sold; on the limits, that Vase is going to Graham for 123, which would complete the Official's set.", map_hl=[4,2], big=True)
say("Noah", "Here's the arithmetic, since it's my job. If I take the throne now, Graham buys that Vase two minutes later and takes it straight off me, with my 290 in his pocket, and I'd have been a very expensive Emperor for one auction. So I'm showing the set and taking the Official's chair instead. Graham, I'm sorry, it's nothing personal. You had four pieces and the best offer at this table. Rich, you keep the throne for now, and you'll need someone honest to collect the tax.")
swap(4, 2, "Noah Tall shows the Scholar's complete set (Customs House, Land Deed Scroll, Dragon Jar, Wooden Bowl, Vase) and 106 grain, after the sixth lot and before Clark's Vase is sold, and replaces Graham Garner as the Official, in Province 2. He keeps his grain and takes all of Graham's 154.5. His Customs House goes back to Province 2's stock; his Deed, Jar, Bowl and Vase move with him to G12, H12, I12 and J12. Graham returns every piece he holds to stock (his Deed, Brush, Jar and Bowl), draws 60 from the Harvest Pile, and plays the Scholar, in Province 4, from now on.",
 hover=TH(("", "Section 11: an upper-class player may replace any other player. Noah's new objective is the Official's, every type but the Customs House, plus 20: he needs a Bamboo Brush. Graham's limit on the Vase still stands, cut to the 60 he now holds."),
          ("Noah", "Taking Graham's seat before the Vase lot sends his four pieces back to stock, so the one man about to take the throne goes back to nothing. I keep my grain and add his to it. I'd rather stand next to the throne with 257 grain and nobody at my heels than sit on it for a few minutes.")),
 at={"LD":"G12", "DJ":"H12", "WB":"I12", "VA":"J12"})
act("Graham held 154.5 when Noah showed his set (151 plus his quarter of the Customs House Noah had just bought), so Noah takes 260.5, not 257.", map_hl=[2])
say("Noah", "Thank you for the correction. That's 260.5, and I'll write down the extra three and a half like every other grain. My four pieces go in a row along the top, G12 to J12, because those are the only squares in this province no soldier can reach this year or next. Rob, I'm telling you now so you don't waste a walk. The lad on I15 can't get there.",
 "Noah: Last year I put my pieces where they looked tidy and Rob carried one off, so this time they go where the reach tables say no boots can land for two rounds. Now I need a Bamboo Brush and a Customs House again, and I have the grain to buy them.")
lot(7, "VA", [B(4,60), B(4,None,"Go on, then. I'll remember the price."), B(3,61)],
 hover=TH(("", "Clark's minimum was 40. On the limits given before the auction the Vase was going to Graham for 123, completing his set; once Noah had taken his seat, Graham's limit was cut to the 60 he now held, and Reina's limit of 100 took it. Paddy's limits, 15 and 28 to keep it from Graham, were never reached."),), at="L11")
act("With the Vase, Reina holds the Gentry's full set: a Wooden Bowl, Customs House, Bamboo Brush, Dragon Jar and Vase, with 81 grain.", map_hl=[3], big=True)
say("Reina", "Rich darling, sit down, I'm not here for your chair this year. Noah, you're one Brush from the full set with two hundred and sixty in your purse, and there's a Brush sitting in Graham's cart. I'd be Empress for exactly one auction. So I'll take the Official's chair, and the purse that goes with it, thank you. You're the Gentry now, darling. The Pile's heavy, the rent's five, and the furrows are friendly.")
swap(3, 2, "Reina Osorio shows the Gentry's complete set (Wooden Bowl, Customs House, Bamboo Brush, Dragon Jar, Vase) and 81 grain, and replaces Noah Tall as the Official, in Province 2. She keeps her grain and takes all of Noah's 260.5. Her Customs House goes back to Province 2's stock; her Bowl, Brush, Jar and Vase move with her to G12, H12, I12 and J12. Noah returns every piece he holds to stock (his Deed, Jar, Bowl and Vase), draws 60 from the Harvest Pile, and plays the Gentry, in Province 3, from now on.",
 hover=TH(("", "Section 11: an upper-class player may replace any other player. Reina's new objective is the Official's, every type but the Customs House, plus 20: she needs a Land Deed Scroll. The Province 2 seat has changed hands twice in one auction."),
          ("Reina", "The throne is only worth having when nobody can take it off you, and Noah was a single cheap Brush away from doing exactly that to whoever sat in it. So I've taken his seat and his 260 instead: I'm on about 340, more than Rich's whole reserve, his set is broken, and his old Deed goes back into the Gentry's stock, where I can buy it.")),
 at={"WB":"G12", "BB":"H12", "DJ":"I12", "VA":"J12"})
say("Reina", "Along the top row, darlings, the same squares Noah picked, because he picked well. Rob, you can tell the lad on I15 to save his boots. And Noah, about the one Land Deed in your cart: when it comes up, I'd much rather we talk about it nicely before the auction than during it.",
 "Reina: Those four squares are the only ones in Province 2 that no soldier can reach this year or next, so my pieces are safe while I wait for a Deed. Noah now holds the only Deed stock at the table, so he'll either sell it to me nicely or I'll outbid him, and with 340 against his 60 we both know how that ends.")
act("Noah's stock as the Gentry holds two Land Deed Scrolls, Graham's and his own, both returned to it this year. Stock beside the board now: Reina 3 Customs Houses, Noah 2 Land Deeds, Graham 2 Brushes, Silke 2 Jars, Paddy 3 Bowls, Clark 2 Vases.", map_hl=[2,3,4,5,6,7])
march({"W1":"I10", "W2":"I14", "W3":"M21", "W4":"Q25", "W5":"P19"}, take={"W4":("DJ","T22")}, text="Robert Land marches: W4 steps onto Q25 in Paddy's Province 6 and takes his Dragon Jar instead of the 5, setting it on T22 in the southern sand. W2 steps onto I14 in Province 2, Reina's now, and W5 onto P19 in Silke's Province 5; Reina and Silke pay 5 each. W1 steps back onto I10 and W3 onto M21, squares already raided.",
 hover="Rob: That's three names crossed off now, and W4 never even broke a sweat. W1 and W2 are closing in on Reina's shiny new row from both sides, and W3 and W5 are walking on Silke's Deed and Brush, so in two more marches the coast and the crown's new Official both get a visit.")
say("Rob", "Paddy, I'm sorry, but you did put it one step from my lad and call it a coastline. It'll look lovely on T22. Reina, darling, the top row's three days off for two of my boys, and you can't fit a whole treasury into one Official's pocket. Silke, you put the new thread three steps away, and three steps is nothing.")
salaries({2:8, 4:6}, text="Salaries: Rich pays the Official 8, now Reina, and the Scholar 6, now Graham, as he declared.")

# ============================== YEAR 6 ==============================
R(6, "The Wall is breached", "breach")
act("Graham, now the Scholar, turns the I Ching card: The Wall Is Breached. Every province collects a good harvest and the population holds at x1, 100 million. Provinces 1, 2 and 3 each pay 5 for Wall repairs, or 2 to the Harvest Pile if no Wall stands above them; Provinces 4 to 8 each play their next personal disaster.", map_hl=[4])
say("Graham", "Year Six. The Wall Is Breached. 'The harvest is in, and so are the horsemen from the north.' Every province collects a good harvest. Three links of the Wall are breached: Provinces 1, 2 and 3 pay 5 each for repairs, or 2 in damages to the Pile if no wall is built above them. Provinces 4 through 8 each play the next Personal Disaster in their booklet, and that includes mine. The counter stays at 100 million, so it's x1 again; read the x1 row. And Rich, I'll take my six at the end of the year. I've had a lesson lately in how quickly a purse can change hands.",
 "Graham: Last year I had four pieces and a hundred and fifty-four grain, and four minutes later I had nothing, and my grain had gone into Reina's purse by way of Noah. Now I start over with sixty-six and the pen that counts everyone else's money. I'm writing every figure down.")
act("The Wall is breached. Rich repairs the four links above Province 1 for 5; no Wall stands above Provinces 2 and 3, so Reina and Noah pay 2 each in damages. All of it goes to the Harvest Pile.", deltas={3:-5, 1:-2, 4:-2}, pile_delta=9, map_hl=[1,2,3])
disaster(4, "the Scholar's translations of the Emperor's foreign books invent so many new words that the Emperor, baffled, docks half of four years' salary.")
disaster(5, "the irrigation ditch the Silk Peasant and her sons built over the years is washed away by a flash flood, and must be rebuilt.")
disaster(6, "fire takes the Rice Peasant's house and grain reserve, and he borrows to get the family through the year.")
disaster(7, "the Wheat Peasant's blacksmith shop catches fire, and the furnace takes two months to fix.")
disaster(8, "one of the Warlord's most trusted lieutenants runs off with his daughter to start a band of raiders. No raids at all this year: he is too busy feasting the newlyweds.")
harvest("Noah, now the Gentry, pays out the good harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
say("Noah", "Sixty each to Provinces 2 to 6, eighty to Clark for the Reservoir, sixty to Rich and twenty to Rob. I've counted it twice, and it comes out of a Pile that's lighter than it was. Rent's five each, the same as Reina charged, and I'll not raise it on anyone who's just had a fire or a flood. For the record, I told you all the throne would be taken off me inside one auction, and then I sat in Graham's chair and forgot the same thing applied to it. That one's my arithmetic, not anyone else's. Reina, you're right about the Deeds. I've two of them in my cart, and you'll find me happy to talk before the auction, at a fair price.",
 "Noah: Two hundred and sixty grain gone in one move, and the lesson is that whoever holds a full set with the last piece showing can take whatever chair you've just sat down in. Now I'm the one holding the only two Land Deeds left, and Reina needs one, so I'm back in the game.")
rents(5, "Rents: Noah, the new Gentry, keeps the minimum, and each peasant pays him 5.")
say("Rich", "Thirty again, my friends. It's moderate, it's civilised, and the Wall has had its five. Paddy, you've lost a house and a Jar in one year, so the crown is digging both canal sections through your fields at its own cost, eighty grain, and that's twenty more grain for you every year from now on. Reina darling, congratulations on the chair, and on the purse. I'll be watching Noah's Deeds with the same interest you are, and I can count to one as well as anyone.",
 "Rich: Reina sits on four hundred grain one Deed short of my throne, and if she gets it she takes my whole reserve with her, so any grain I put into the ground tonight is grain she can't carry off. The canal takes 80 off the 690 owed and gives me a grateful Paddy, and grateful peasants make useful bidders when a Land Deed comes up.")
taxes(.30, collector="Reina, now the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
act("Robert Land doesn't pay his 6.", map_hl=[8])
say("Reina", "Thirty percent, darlings, counted twice like Graham taught us: eighteen each from Noah, Graham, Silke, Paddy and me, because the Official pays her own share, and twenty-four from Clark for the Reservoir. That's a hundred and fourteen, and it all goes to the crown. Rob, you owe six this year on top of the arrears, and I'm writing it down in ink. And Noah, darling, since we're talking before the auction: I'll pay thirty for one of your Deeds, in public, and nobody else needs to get bruised over it.",
 "Reina: An honest collection keeps Rich content and gives no peasant a reason to sign anything against me, and that's worth far more to me than skimming a few grain. The offer to Noah is out in the open on purpose: with two Deeds in his cart he can sell me one and still have one to sell, and if anyone tries to bid me up, they'll be doing it against 400 grain.")
build("Grand Canal", 80, {1:80}, canal={6:2}, text="Public works: Rich digs both sections of the Grand Canal in Paddy's Province 6, at the crown's cost of 80. Each adds 10 to Paddy's harvest from next year.", hover="Rich pays the 80, into the Harvest Pile. The Winter Palace, which the canal needs first, already stands. 610 of the works are still owed.")
act("The auctions: one lot from each seller, in order. Reina now sells Province 2's Customs Houses, Noah Province 3's Land Deeds and Graham Province 4's Brushes; Noah has two Deeds, but sells one a year.", map_hl=[2,3,4,5,6,7])
say("Reina", "My Customs House starts at five, darlings, and I'm bidding on only one thing this year, Noah's Deed. Anyone who wants to bid against me for it is welcome to try, but I'd ask Graham how a hundred and twenty-three for a Vase worked out for him. Noah, your quarter will be a nice one, I promise.",
 "Reina: Nobody else who needs a Deed has more than 145, so a ceiling of 160 wins it whatever happens, and I'm left with well over the 20 I need to show the set. I'm passing on everything else because the only lot this year that matters to me is the Deed.")
say("Noah", "Reina, that's a kind offer and I'll be as honest about it as you were. You hold four of five and 384.5 grain. The Deed is your fifth, and the throne pays you Rich's 259.5 on top. Thirty for a Deed is thirty for the crown, and I won't sell the crown for thirty. So my Deed starts at 365 this year. You'd have to keep 20 back, which leaves you 364.5, half a grain short, and I worked that out before I said it. I'm not hiding anything: I'll talk about a price every year, in public, and it'll be a price for a throne, not for a scroll. For the rest, I'm bidding small and I'll say so: fifteen at most on anything, twelve on the Bowl.",
 "Noah: I let one full set walk past me last year, and I won't let another one through my own cart for thirty grain. A minimum half a grain above what Reina can pay keeps the last Deed out of her hands for one more year.")
say("Graham", "I've got ninety-eight and an empty shelf, so I'm doing it the slow way: small bids, and nothing over sixteen on anything. My Brush starts at five. Noah, three hundred and sixty-five is the most honest figure anyone's said at this table all night. I'm not bidding on your Deed, and I'd like it noted that the man who paid a hundred and twenty-three for a Vase has learned something.",
 "Graham: Last year one lot took everything I had, so this year I've spread it out: four modest ceilings and no bidding wars. Reina is the one to watch now. She needs one Deed, and Noah just priced it at the cost of a throne.")
act("Graham never paid the 123 for the Vase: once Noah took his seat, his limit was cut to the 60 he held, and Reina took it for 61.", map_hl=[4])
say("Silke", "I lost a ditch to the flood and I'm down to ninety-five, so I'll put it plainly. I need a Customs House and a Bowl and nothing else in this world. Noah, Graham, you've both told us your numbers, and mine are higher. Clark, you're the one with a full purse. If you want to bruise me on these two you can, but the Pile keeps three quarters of every bruise. Reina, Noah, take your Deed somewhere quiet, I'm not in it.",
 "Silke: Forty and thirty-five comes to seventy-five, and that leaves exactly the twenty I have to keep back. If both of them land, the Silk Peasant shows a full set this very year, and the purse I take will be a good deal fatter than mine.")
say("Paddy", "Well, I've a fire, a lost Jar and two canals in one year, and I'm still not sure which one to thank Rich for. Thank you, Rich, I mean it, those ditches will feed me for years. Noah, three hundred and sixty-five is a fine price for a scroll, and I'll not be paying it, so you two can talk all you like. For the rest I'm starting again from an empty shelf, so I'll bid a little past the fifteens and no further. My Bowls start at five, same as ever.",
 "Paddy: Noah and Graham have both told us they stop at fifteen or sixteen, so a ceiling of seventeen or eighteen should get me a piece or two cheap without starting a fight, and I'll have sixty-odd left for the new land. The Deed's priced for Reina's throne, not for me, so I'm keeping out of it.")
say("Clark", "For the minutes: I'm still out of the Jars. I promised to stay out until Reina and Noah each had one, and Noah's lost his along with his chair, so that promise stands. Noah, three hundred and sixty-five for a Deed is the best piece of leverage I've seen all night, and it's going in the ledger under 'admired'. Silke, I'll bid on the Customs House and the Bowl like any farmer with an empty shelf. I'm not out to bruise you, but I'm not standing aside for nothing either.",
 "Clark: My Vase fetched a hundred and twenty-three on paper and two thrones changed hands over it, so it isn't worth gouging anyone this year. I'm bidding steadily on the pieces nobody's fighting over and leaving Noah's three-hundred-and-sixty-five-grain Deed for Reina to choke on.")
say("Rob", "A lieutenant's run off with my daughter, so the lads are on compassionate leave this year. Bid away, all of you. Graham, do buy something, anything at all. You're the only one left with nothing for me to take, and it's starting to feel rude.",
 "Rob: I can't raid this year, so I'm sitting on a hundred and seventy grain and letting them fill the shelves. Graham's on my list and he's got nothing on the board, so whatever he buys this year, he's just buying it for me to collect.")
act("Minimums: Reina's Customs House 5, Noah's Land Deed 365 (the price he named above), Graham's Brush 5, Silke's Jar 10, Paddy's Bowl 5, Clark's Vase 10. Rob passes on every lot. Three lots come out close, the Brush (Clark 18, Paddy 17), the Jar and the Vase (Paddy 17, Graham 16 on each), and the top two on each get one live raise.", map_hl=[2,3,4,5,6,7,8])
say("Clark", "Paddy, I'll go three more for the Brush and not a grain past that. There's a second one in Graham's cart, so neither of us needs to bleed over this one, and the minutes will show I said so first.",
 "Clark: A one-grain lead is how I lost a chair before, so I'm adding three for safety; past that, the second Brush in stock makes it cheaper to wait.")
say("Paddy", "Clark, Graham, I'm sorry, lads, but my shelf's as empty as yours and I've a house to put back up. Twenty-two and not one grain more, on all three.",
 "Paddy: Twenty-two a piece is still cheap for a fresh start, and even if I win all three I've sixty-odd left over for the new land.")
say("Graham", "Twenty on the Jar and twenty on the Vase, Paddy, and that's where I stop. We've both got empty shelves, and I'd rather not have the Pile do well out of the two of us.",
 "Graham: Two pieces at twenty each still leaves me sixty to rebuild with, and I won't be fighting over anything again.")
DC = "Noted, and carried forward to next year's page."
DN = "That's past fifteen, so it's yours. I'm keeping my grain this year."
DP = "That's past what a rice farmer can pay. Go on, it's yours."
DG = "Go on, then. I'll remember the price, and I'm not paying it this time."
lot(2, "CH", [B(4,15), B(4,None,DN), B(2,16), B(2,None,DG), B(6,18), B(6,None,DP), B(7,22), B(7,None,DC), B(5,23)],
 hover="Reina's minimum was 5. Silke puts the Customs House on M25.", at="M25")
act("Noah's Land Deed Scroll opens at 365 and draws no bid. Both Deeds stay in his cart.", map_hl=[3])
lot(4, "BB", [B(3,15), B(3,None,DN), B(7,21), B(7,None,DC), B(6,22)],
 hover="Graham's minimum was 5. In the live raise Clark went to 21 and Paddy to 22. Paddy puts the Brush on S19.", at="S19")
lot(5, "DJ", [B(3,15), B(3,None,DN), B(4,20), B(4,None,DG), B(6,21)],
 hover="Silke's minimum was 10. Clark stayed out, as he had promised. In the live raise Graham went to 20 and Paddy to 22. Paddy puts the Jar on S20.", at="S20")
lot(6, "WB", [B(3,12), B(3,None,DN), B(4,13), B(4,None,DG), B(7,16), B(7,None,DC), B(5,17)],
 hover="Paddy's minimum was 5. Silke puts the Bowl on N25.", at="N25")
act("With the Customs House and the Bowl, Silke holds the Silk Peasant's full set: a Vase, Land Deed Scroll, Bamboo Brush, Customs House and Wooden Bowl, with 60 grain. It is complete after the sixth lot; the last lot, Clark's Vase, goes to Paddy and completes no one's set.", map_hl=[5], big=True)
say("Silke", "Vase, Deed, Brush, Customs House, Bowl, and twenty grain. Five years of losing every lot, and then two years of winning the ones that mattered. Reina darling, you told Graham to count his thirty carefully, so I'll count yours: three hundred and ninety-one. I'll take the chair and the purse, thank you. The coast is yours now, and the rent is five, and the sea air is very good for pottery.")
swap(5, 2, "Silke Weber shows the Silk Peasant's complete set (Vase, Land Deed Scroll, Bamboo Brush, Customs House, Wooden Bowl) and 60 grain, and replaces Reina Osorio as the Official, in Province 2. She keeps her grain and takes all of Reina's 391. Her Customs House goes back to Province 2's stock; her Vase, Deed, Brush and Bowl move with her to G13, G14, G15 and G16. Reina returns every piece she holds to stock (her Bowl, Brush, Jar and Vase), draws 60 from the Harvest Pile, and plays the Silk Peasant, in Province 5, from now on.",
 hover=TH(("", "Section 11: a peasant may replace the Official, Gentry, Scholar or another peasant, never the Emperor. Silke's new objective is the Official's, every type but the Customs House, plus 20: she needs a Dragon Jar. The Province 2 seat has now changed hands four times in two years."),
          ("Silke", "I picked the fattest purse at the table and the one player who could have bought Noah's crown-priced Deed next year. Now she has sixty grain and an empty shelf, and I'm the one counting the tax.")),
 at={"VA":"G13", "LD":"G14", "BB":"G15", "WB":"G16"})
say("Silke", "Down the west edge in a column, Rob, where Graham kept his once, and nobody walked off with them in a year when your lads are on leave. I need one Dragon Jar, and there happen to be two in my old cart. Paddy, you're the Silk Peasant now, so I'll be bidding on your Jars, and I'll pay a fair price for one in public.",
 "Silke: Every square in Province 2 is open to Rob next year, so I've put them in the column furthest from his lads and I'll want that Jar before his boys are back from leave. One piece and twenty grain off a full set, with nearly four hundred in my purse. The weaver's sitting at the top table now.")
act("Reina, not Paddy, is the Silk Peasant now, so the two Jars in Province 5's stock are hers to sell. Silke holds 451.", map_hl=[5])
lot(7, "VA", [B(3,15), B(3,None,DN), B(4,20), B(4,None,DG), B(6,21)],
 hover="Clark's minimum was 10. In the live raise Graham went to 20 and Paddy to 22. Paddy puts the Vase on S21.", at="S21")
say("Paddy", "Three pieces for sixty-four grain, and I'm not sure I've ever had a better day at market. They're going in a row at the back by the paddies, Rob, well away from the sand, so you can tell your lads not to bother.",
 "Paddy: I learned my lesson with that Jar on the coast. S19, S20 and S21 are out of every soldier's reach this year and next, so this time they'll stay where I put them.")
march({"W1":"I11", "W3":"L22"}, text="Robert Land marches, raiding nowhere this year while he feasts the newlyweds: W1 steps onto I11 and W3 back onto L22, squares already raided. W2, W4 and W5 stand still.",
 hover="Rob: Paddy's off my list, so his new row is safe. Silke's column is two steps from W2 and W1's coming down behind him. Graham and Reina have empty shelves, so W3 is drifting toward Rich's Winter Palace on H21, in case one of them never buys anything worth taking.")
say("Rob", "Down the west edge in a column, Silke? Where Graham kept his? Darling, that's the most famous shop window in China. W2 is two steps from every piece you own, and he's only on leave for one more round. Paddy, I've already had a Jar off you, so you can stop hiding the Vase. I'm full up on paddy pottery.")
salaries({2:8, 4:6}, text="Salaries: Rich pays the Official 8, now Silke, and the Scholar 6, Graham, as he declared.")

# ===== CHAPTER 4: Paddy, Settled
# ============================== YEAR 7 ==============================
R(7, "Imperial charity", "chest")
act("Graham, the Scholar, turns the I Ching card: Imperial Charity. Every province collects an average harvest, the population holds at x1, 100 million, and the Emperor gives every player 2 from his reserve.", map_hl=[4])
say("Graham", "\"Imperial Charity. Moved by the suffering of his people, the Emperor opens his purse. Slightly.\" Every province gets an average harvest, and every player gets two from the Emperor's reserve. Population holds at a hundred million, so that's times one; read the x1 row. No disasters this year. And Rich, since we're on charity: the Scholar's salary is five at the least, and that would be five on top of the two.",
 "Graham: Two grain each from a man holding two hundred and forty-five. I'll write it down as generosity, since that's what the card calls it, and an average year at x1 means nobody's land grows, so the auction is the only place left to get ahead.")
harvest("Noah, the Gentry, pays out the average harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
act("Imperial Charity: Rich gives 2 from the reserve to each of the seven other players, 14 in all.", deltas={3:-14, 1:2, 2:2, 4:2, 5:2, 6:2, 7:2, 8:2}, map_hl=[1])
say("Noah", "Average harvest all round, so here's the sum. Thirty-five each to Rich, Silke, Graham, Reina and me, fifty-five each to Paddy and Clark, and ten to Rob. That's two hundred and ninety-five out of the Pile, which leaves five hundred and thirty-three. Rent stays at five, same as last year: Reina, Paddy, Clark, five each please. And Paddy, I've still got two Deeds in the cart and I still owe you a favour, so when it's your turn come and talk to me about a fair price. I mean a fair one this time, not three hundred and sixty-five.",
 "Noah: Fifteen in rent and my thirty-five takes me to two hundred and two. Silke is sitting on nearly five hundred and only needs a Jar, so I'd much rather Paddy has my Deed than see the whole table wait for her to take the throne.")
act("Paddy and Clark collect 55, not 35: Paddy's two canal sections and Clark's Reservoir add 20 each. The Harvest Pile held 1123 before the harvest, so it holds 828 after it, not 533.", map_hl=[3])
rents(5, "Rents: Noah keeps the rent at 5, and each peasant pays him 5.")
say("Rich", "Thirty percent, my friends. Moderate, civilised and not a grain more, as always. Graham, your five is eight, minus the two the card already handed you, so call it six and a warm smile. And this year the crown digs both canal sections through Province 5, all eighty at my own expense. Reina, your harvest is twenty a year fatter from today. Think of it as a gift from a grateful sovereign to the lady who once held his fields.",
 "Rich: Eighty grain is cheap if it keeps a Dragon Jar out of Silke's hands. I haven't asked Reina for a thing, and that's why she'll remember it the next time Silke comes to her cart with four hundred and ninety-six in her purse.")
taxes(.30, collector="Silke, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
act("Robert Land doesn't pay his 3.", map_hl=[8])
say("Silke", "Thirty percent it is, and I'll put mine in first so nobody says the collector skimmed. Ten and a half from me, Noah, Graham and Reina; sixteen and a half from Paddy and Clark. Seventy-five on the nail, Your Majesty, counted twice. And Rob, I'll ask you politely, once, in front of witnesses. And Rich, thank you for the eight. I'll be keeping the receipt.",
 "Silke: Eighty grain of canal dug through Reina's fields, the year after I took her chair: Rich isn't being generous, he's buying the goodwill of the one woman selling Dragon Jars. I'm the one person at this table who needs a Jar, so I'm watching that canal very closely.")
build("Grand Canal", 80, {1:80}, canal={5:2}, text="Public works: Rich digs both sections of the Grand Canal in Reina's Province 5, at the crown's cost of 80. Each adds 10 to Reina's harvest from next year.", hover="Rich pays the 80, into the Harvest Pile. The Winter Palace, which the canal needs first, already stands. 530 of the works are still owed.")
act("The auctions: one lot from each seller, in order. Silke sells Province 2's Customs Houses, Noah Province 3's Land Deeds, Graham Province 4's Brushes, Reina Province 5's Dragon Jars, Paddy Province 6's Bowls and Clark Province 7's Vases.", map_hl=[2,3,4,5,6,7])
say("Silke", "Ten for my Customs House, and it's cheap at that. Everyone knows which lot I'm sitting up for, so I'll say it plainly: I want Reina's Jar, and anyone who wants to make me pay for it is pouring their own grain into Noah's Pile to do it. Reina, the sea air must have done wonders for your pottery.",
 "Silke: One Jar and I've a full set and the purse to show it, and the only person at this table with more than a hundred and eighty-five grain is me. So I bid high and early, and let them work out how much it costs to try stopping me.")
act("Rich holds 261.5, but the Emperor doesn't bid at auction.", map_hl=[1])
say("Noah", "First, I got two sums wrong a minute ago, and the Storyteller's right on both: the Pile is eight hundred and twenty-eight, and I was on a hundred and sixty-seven before the tax. I'm on a hundred and fifty-six and a half now. My Deed's minimum is eight, Paddy, and that's the fair price I promised you. I'm not standing in Silke's way on the Jar either. Nobody here can outbid four hundred and eighty-five, and trying would only feed the Pile.",
 "Noah: I need all five pieces, so I'm buying cheap ones and not getting into any bidding wars. If I set the Deed at eight, Paddy gets it and I've paid off the favour I've owed him since Year One.")
say("Graham", "Six and a warm smile, Rich. I'll take the six, and I'll write the smile down at its market value. The Brush opens at six. As for the Jar, Silke, it's yours. I've learned what it costs to argue with four hundred and eighty-five, and I've already paid that lesson once.",
 "Graham: Small bids, a firm ceiling on each, and nothing that leaves me unable to buy land when the population doubles. If I lose every lot at these prices, I still come out of the auctions with grain, and that's a better result than I've managed for two years.")
say("Reina", "Silke darling, nobody's standing in your way, and I wouldn't dream of it. The Jar opens at a hundred and fifty. The sea air is lovely, but it doesn't pay for itself, and you did walk off with three hundred and ninety-one of mine. Call it the civilised conversation I promised you, settled in public with the minutes as witness. As for everyone else, I'm only shopping for the cheap pieces this year. I'm starting again from the bottom, and the bottom is a very good place to plan from.",
 "Reina: She'll pay it, because there isn't another Jar for sale anywhere, so a hundred and fifty out of her purse does more to slow her down than any bid I could make. Meanwhile I pick up whatever nobody else is fighting over, and with Rich's canal coming in next year, I'm rebuilding a set one cheap lot at a time, the same way Rich built his.")
act("Reina's second Jar stays in her cart, for next year's auction: a seller puts up one piece a year.", map_hl=[5])
say("Paddy", "Eight's a fair price, Noah, and I'll hold you to the spirit of it, even if the lads with deeper pockets don't. I'll be honest with the table, since I can't hide it: I'm after the Customs House and the Deed, and nothing else. My Bowl opens at five, and it's a good honest bowl, Reina, if you're shopping from the bottom.",
 "Paddy: Two pieces and I've a whole set, which I'd not have dreamed of in Year One, so I'm putting nearly everything on those two lots. If Clark or Graham want to empty their purses into Noah's Pile to stop me, they can, but they'll feel it at tax time.")
say("Clark", "For the minutes: I'm still out of the Jars, since my promise stands until Noah has one again. Paddy, here's a deal in public so nobody feeds the Pile. You have Noah's Deed at his fair eight and I won't lift a finger, and I take the Customs House. If you bid against me on it anyway, that goes in my ledger, and you've seen the size of my ledger. My Vase opens at ten, and it's a modest price from a modest wheat farmer.",
 "Clark: Last year I lost every lot by one grain, so this year I've given up the Deed to buy Paddy's goodwill and put real money behind the Customs House, the Brush and the Bowl. Three pieces off the shelf and I'm suddenly the wheat farmer everyone should have been watching. Noted, and carried forward.")
say("Rob", "Not a grain from the desert this year, friends. Graham, Reina, the cheap pieces are going for a song. Buy something nice and put it somewhere I can admire it. I'll be along to have a look.",
 "Rob: I don't need a single pot. What I need is for Graham and Reina to have one on the board, so the best thing I can do at this auction is stay out of their way and keep the prices low.")
say("Paddy", "That's a fair offer, Clark, and I'll shake on it with one small addition so neither of us ends up a piece short. You take the Customs House today and I take the Deed. Next year you leave the next Customs House to me and I leave Noah's last Deed to you. That way we both finish, and none of it goes into the Pile.",
 "Paddy: Clark's got near twice my grain, so I'd likely lose the Customs House to him anyway. This way I get the Deed at Noah's eight, and Clark's promise gets me first go at the next Customs House.")
say("Clark", "Done, Paddy, and shaken on in front of witnesses. The Customs House is mine today, the Deed is yours, and next year we swap. I'm writing it on a fresh page headed 'Paddy, settled', which is the first page in that ledger that isn't a grievance.",
 "Clark: A Customs House with no rival this year and a clear run at Noah's last Deed next year, and the Pile gets none of it. Two pieces in two years without a fight is the best leverage a wheat farmer has had all game.")
act("A deal in public: Paddy stands aside on the Customs House and Clark on the Deed this year; next year Clark stands aside on the Customs House and Paddy on the Deed.", map_hl=[6,7])
act("Minimums: Silke's Customs House 10, Noah's Land Deed 8, Graham's Brush 6, Reina's Dragon Jar 150, Paddy's Bowl 5, Clark's Vase 10. Rob passes on every lot. No lot comes out close enough for a live raise.", map_hl=[2,3,4,5,6,7,8])
DN7 = "That's past my number. It's yours, and good luck to you."
DG7 = "That's past my figure. It's yours, and I'll remember what you paid."
DR7 = "Go on then, darling, it's yours. I'll catch the next one."
