say("Silke", "Four pieces for seventy-one grain, and I won two of them by one grain this time, so the thread has finally come round my way. Your Majesty, congratulations, and the Scholar will read your cards just as faithfully as she read Graham's. Rich, I'll be wanting one of those Deeds next spring, and I hope the price comes down from fifty.",
 "Silke: The Jar went on K19 because it's the one square Rob's lads can't reach next year either, and the other three are tucked inland where no boots can reach them this year. I'm one Deed from a full set, Rich sells the Deeds and has thirteen grain, so he and I will be having a very friendly conversation before next spring. (From the board: Silke won all four lots by one grain, and Rich holds 25.5.)")
say("Clark", "Ten grain for the Scholar's Brush, which is her own opening price, so Silke, that's one lot you didn't lose by one. It goes on P26, down at the far end of the silk coast, and Rob, your lads are welcome to walk there if they bring a packed lunch. Your Majesty Reina, congratulations, and I've written it on 'Reina, admired and owed' and underlined the 'admired'.",
 "Clark: P26 is out of every soldier's reach this year and next, so the Brush is the first piece I've had that's safe from W4 since I moved to the coast. That's two of five. Silke needs only a Deed now, so she's the one I'm watching this year, and it isn't Reina.")
march({"W1":"J13", "W2":"J14", "W3":"I20", "W4":"K24", "W5":"M20"}, text="Robert Land marches: W1 onto J13 and W2 onto J14 in Province 2 (Graham, the new Official, pays 5 each); W3 onto I20 in Province 1 (Reina pays 10); W4 onto K24 and W5 onto M20 in Province 4 (Silke pays 5 each).",
 hover="Rob: Reina's set went back to stock when she took the throne, so now I need two of Silke, Rich and Graham. W3 and W5 are each one step from Silke's new row, and W1 and W2 are heading down the column to Rich's Bowl on J17, so this could be the year the desert finishes the set.")
say("Rob", "Congratulations, Your Majesty Reina. You took your treasures off the shelf just as my lads arrived to dust them, so I'll call that a draw. Silke, darling, you've stood four new pots in your front garden, and two of my boys are already at the gate. Rich, keep the kettle on, there are two more coming down the road to see that Bowl.")
salaries({2:8, 4:8}, text="Salaries: Reina, the Emperor at the end of the year, pays what Graham declared: 8 to the Official, now Graham, and 8 to the Scholar, Silke.")



# ===== CHAPTER 7: Thirty Grain and a Shovel
# ============================== YEAR 13 ==============================
R(13, "A bad harvest", "dribble2")
act("Silke, the Scholar, turns the I Ching card: Bad Harvest. Every province collects a bad harvest. The population stays at x3, 300 million.", map_hl=[4])
say("Silke", "\"Bad Harvest. The seed rotted, the rains came late, and the ox went lame.\" All provinces take a bad harvest this year. The counter stays at three hundred million, so that's times three. Read the x3 row, everyone, and the bad-harvest column. Rich, a thin year for the Pile is a thin year for whoever holds it, so I'd sell a Deed while somebody can still pay for one.",
 "Silke: A bad harvest means every purse at this table is about to get thinner, and the thinner Rich gets, the more one Land Deed is worth to him in cash right now. I'm one Deed short of a full set, and Noah wants the same Deed, so I'd like it settled before he's done counting his rice. (Silke whispers to Rich an offer of 25 for a Deed before the auction. Unsold stock is sold only at auction, so the Storyteller tells them both it can't be done privately.)")
harvest("Rich, the Gentry, pays out a bad harvest at x3. Most incomes are negative, and those are paid into the Harvest Pile.")
say("Rich", "A thin year, my friends, and a thin year needs a gentle landlord. Five apiece, as always, and not a grain more. Clark, Noah, Paddy, you'll find me the most reasonable man in the north-west. As for the Deeds, Silke, they go in the cart like everything else, and the price is what it was.",
 "Rich: Fifteen in rent puts me at fifty and a half if the game ends this year, and every grain counts as a tiebreaker. Silke is one Deed short of a set, and I'm not going to sell her Reina's four hundred and seventy-four for twenty-five. (Rich whispers back to Silke that no Deed of his opens under fifty, as he promised Graham, and that she may meet fifty in the cart.)")
rents(5, "Rents: Rich keeps the rent at 5, and each peasant pays him 5.")
say("Reina", "A bad harvest, darlings, so the tax is twenty, the gentlest rate in the book, and Graham's salary and Silke's stay at eight, as promised. And the crown keeps its promises: the Irrigation System for Province 2 goes in this year, all thirty out of the treasury and not a grain from you, Graham. The minutes will say the Empress finished every work she was owed.",
 "Reina: That's the last work owed, and the game ends the moment it's built, before anyone gets to wheel a cart into the auction hall. I've spent twelve years losing chairs at auction, so this year there isn't going to be one.")
act("Reina declares: tax 20%; the Province 2 Irrigation System, the last work owed, at the crown's whole cost of 30; salaries of 8 each to the Official and the Scholar at the year's end; and half of every square of new land for the other provinces.", map_hl=[1,2])
say("Graham", "Twenty percent, collected in full. Clark eighteen, Noah fourteen, I pay my own ten, Paddy six, Rich two, Silke nothing, and that's fifty for the crown. Rob, I've written your refusal down. And so the minutes are accurate, Majesty: thirty is the last work owed. When it goes in, the game ends before the auction, and whoever has the most grain is holding the throne at that moment. A gift to Province 2 that closes every other shelf at this table isn't a gift to Province 2.",
 "Graham: She doesn't want my canal, she wants the bell rung before Silke can buy a Deed. I can't stop her digging it, but I can make sure everyone at this table knows what the shovel is for. (The work is the Province 2 Irrigation System. Rob's income is negative this year, so he owes no tax to refuse.)")
taxes(.20, collector="Graham, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
say("Graham", "G12 and G13, at the back, where no soldier has ever bothered to stand. I won't be buying any land this year, thank you. Majesty, it's a fine canal. Nobody here will live long enough to drink from it.",
 "Graham: The ditch goes on the quietest squares I've got, and I keep all ninety-eight grain, because if the game ends tonight the grain is the only score I have left. She's ending it to keep the throne she's sitting on, and I'm not going to pretend that's generosity.")
build("Province 2 Irrigation System", 30, {1:30}, key="irr2", at="G12:G13", text="Public works: Reina builds the Province 2 Irrigation System, one square by two, on G12 and G13 in Graham's Province 2, at the crown's whole cost of 30. Graham chose the site; under this year's ruling the crown needs no agreement to build a work it pays for in full.", hover="Reina pays the 30, into the Harvest Pile. It is the last public work owed.")
assert all_built(), "not every public work is built"
act("Every public work the country owes is standing: three palaces, twelve links of the Great Wall, eight sections of the Grand Canal, the irrigation system and the two reservoirs, 1,030 in all. The game ends here, before the auction: the players have survived, and the Empress's reign has been a glorious one.", map_hl=list(range(1,9)), big=True)
say("Reina", "Graham darling, you're quite right, nobody will drink from it, but it's a lovely canal, and every work this country was owed is standing. Thirteen years, and I lost a chair in that auction hall five times, so I thought I'd spare you all one more afternoon of it. Thank you for a very civilised game, every one of you, and Rich, the minutes will say you started it.",
 "Reina: I planned years ahead for thirteen years, and in the end the plan that counted was keeping the auction hall shut. The Empress was watching every cart, and this time she made sure there weren't any.")
say("Graham", "Ninety-eight grain, an empty shelf, and a canal on G12 named after me. Majesty, a well-run reign, and you ended it at exactly the right moment, which is the one skill none of the rest of us had. Thirteen years. I sat in four chairs and lost three of them to a full set shown on the table. I'll remember the price, and I'm glad I paid it.",
 "Graham: I counted every grain anyone ever took from me, and she was the one who worked out you can stop the count. Fifth on grain, the Official again, and the most honest set of figures at this table.")
say("Rich", "Majesty, a glorious reign, and closing it before the auction was the most elegant move of the evening. I'd have done the same, and I'd have smiled while I did it. I started this game as the Gentry with sixty grain and I'm finishing it as the Gentry with forty-eight and a half, so let the minutes say I kept the seat I drew and broke my word to no one. Thank you all. It's been an education, and I'm afraid most of it came from me.",
 "Rich: Last at the table, but I'm back in the seat I drew, and every promise I made held to the letter, including the one that cost me. Reina learned the most important trick of all, which is knowing when the game should stop, and I never got to teach her that one.")
say("Silke", "Majesty, I read the card that said the harvest would be bad, and I never thought to read the treasury. Thirty grain to close the book, and you closed it with four of my five pieces on the table. A well-run reign, and I mean it. I drew the Scholar and I'm finishing as the Scholar, but in between I sat on the throne and in the Official's chair, and I paid a hundred and fifty for a Jar without blinking. Rich, fifty was the price, and you kept your word to the letter, as you said in Year 1. I'll remember the letter.",
 "Silke: One Land Deed short, for the second time in this game. The thread came round my way at last, and Reina cut it before the auction began. She had the sharpest eye at the table tonight, and I hope I was a close second. (From the record: Silke drew the Silk Peasant's seat, Province 5, so finishing as the Scholar is a win.)")
say("Clark", "Majesty, I drew the Official's pen and I'm leaving with a silk farmer's hoe and a hundred and a half grain, and every chair I lost along the way went to a full set on the table, which is fair, and I've got all of it written down. Thirteen years: I paid eighteen this harvest and I paid in full every year, and Rob paid nothing, so page one closes on line thirteen. Graham, you get your 'generous' page for the sea, Paddy, you're still 'settled', and Reina, you finish where you've been since Year 9, under 'admired and owed', with 'admired' underlined three times now. You closed the ledger before I could cash it, and that's the best leverage I saw all night.",
 "Clark: I came in with the pen and I'm going out with a Vase, a Brush and seven squares of sea that'll never pay a harvest now. The lesson's the same one I wrote three times: the Empress showed her hand before anyone else could, and she got to decide when the game stopped. (From the board: the seven squares, bought in Year 12, paid into this year's harvest; Province 5 took +90 in a bad year.)")
say("Noah", "Majesty, a glorious reign, and I'll say the sum out loud one last time, because somebody has to. I drew the Scholar's chair with fifty-three and a half grain, and I'm leaving as the Rice Peasant with a hundred and forty-five and a half, three pieces nobody could reach on S23 to S25, and a Deed and a Jar I never got. That's down one chair and up ninety-two grain, and most of the lots I lost, I lost by exactly one grain. Silke, neither of us got that Deed, so my diary's last promise is the only one at this table that was never tested. Thank you all. It was an honest game, and I mean most of you.",
 "Noah: Three times I sat on a full purse and lost the chair under it, so this time I sat on a full purse with no chair to lose, and the Empress closed the book before anyone could take it from me. It wasn't clever, it was arithmetic, and for once the arithmetic was on my side. (From the record: Noah drew the Scholar, Province 4, and finishes as the Rice Peasant, Province 6, two seats down; every player started with 60.)")
say("Paddy", "Majesty, a lovely canal, and a fair finish. I sat down a wheat farmer with an empty cart, and I'm getting up a wheat farmer with a Bowl, a Brush, four squares of new land and fifty-two and a half grain. But for one year in the middle I had the whole set bar a Bowl and Rich's chair to show for it, and nobody can take that out of the minutes. Thank you all. Noah, we're square on that favour, and Clark, I'll take 'settled', it's more than my da ever got written about him.",
 "Paddy: I said next time I got close I'd show straight away, and the game ended before I got close, so I suppose I kept my word. Reina read the treasury while the rest of us were reading the auction list, and that's the lesson I'm taking home with the Bowl. (From the record: Paddy drew the Rice Peasant, Province 6, and finishes as the Wheat Peasant, Province 7. \"Rich's chair\" is the Gentry's seat, which Paddy held in Years 8 and 9.)")
say("Rob", "Majesty, thirteen years and you finished it with thirty grain and a shovel, one march before my lads got to Silke's front garden. W3 and W5 were close enough to read the price tags. I drew the sand and I'm leaving on the sand, with a hundred and seventy-eight and a half grain, three ornaments on the mantel, a palace I visited once, and not one lot bought in thirteen years. Clark, page one, line thirteen: refused, in that lovely hand. Thank you all, it's been a pleasure being the villain.",
 "Rob: Two names short, so the book stays open, and the Empress shut it from her side by building a canal nobody will drink from. I came second in grain without paying for a single pot, and I'll take that home.")

RESULT = export(
 "Every public work was built, so this is the first ending: the players survived and the Empress's reign was a glorious one. Players are ranked by the seat they finished in against the one they drew, with grain breaking ties.",
 ["Reina drew the throne, lost it to Rich in Year 2, and spent ten years climbing back through the Gentry's, the Official's and the Silk Peasant's seats. She took it again from Graham in Year 12 with a Deed bought at Rich's fifty, and in Year 13 the crown built the last work, the Province 2 Irrigation System, alone and before the auction, which by Section 18 is a win for the Emperor.",
  "All 1,030 of the works were built in thirteen years, one project a year, under four Emperors: Rich, Silke, Graham and Reina. When the population rose to x1.5 in Year 11 and then x3 in Year 12, incomes went negative and the table bought thirty-four squares of new land in one season, the crown paying most of it; the reserve fell from 634 to 6, and the land paid for the last harvest.",
  "Seats changed hands in every year from Year 4 on, each time to a complete set shown at the auction. Graham climbed from the wheat fields to the Official's chair and Silke from silk to the Scholar's; Rich ended in the Gentry's seat he drew. Clark, Noah and Paddy each finished lower than they started, Noah two seats down with the third-largest purse.",
  "Rob never bought a lot in thirteen years. He raided three pieces and the Winter Palace, the palace standing in for one name, and finished two names short of his objective, with W3 and W5 one step from Silke's row and the second-largest purse at the table."])
